Historical C2 workaround, replaced by Runtime/ReactOS-cc-bcb-ownership.patch.
Not selected by Configure.cmd. FastFAT keeps its original FAT scan.
The independent fastfat-clean-after-flush (F1) patch remains active.

Finish a whole-volume flush when the last FAT pin has been released.
On 4fbbefd614b x64 Debug, a successful NtFlushBuffersFile left both copies
of FAT32 sector 544 at their old contents. All four uncached whole-disk
reads completed with 512 bytes. The allocation's pinned sample matched the
eventual disk contents; the two writes did not complete until after return.
With this change both writes completed inside the same volume flush and
both immediate reads matched the pinned sample. On x86 and x64 MSVC the
same 16 whole-volume regression cases went from 5/16 to 16/16.

Keep the original FAT pin protocol. Flush only remaining DirtyFatMcb runs
on the volume path; the paging write owns their removal. Propagate cache
errors and refuse success if a previously flushed range is still pending.
This is not a repair for damaged disks or a change to general CC/MM policy.

diff --git a/drivers/filesystems/fastfat/flush.c b/drivers/filesystems/fastfat/flush.c
--- a/drivers/filesystems/fastfat/flush.c
+++ b/drivers/filesystems/fastfat/flush.c
@@ -1019,6 +1019,44 @@
 
     Status = FatFlushFat( IrpContext, Vcb );
 
+    if (NT_SUCCESS(Status)) {
+
+        VBO Vbo;
+        LBO Lbo;
+        ULONG ByteCount;
+        LARGE_INTEGER Offset;
+        LONGLONG FlushedThrough = 0;
+        IO_STATUS_BLOCK Iosb;
+
+        //
+        //  In ReactOS the last unpin can discard the BCB while its data
+        //  is still dirty.  Finish the whole-volume flush using the FAT
+        //  sectors still owed to disk.  Entry 0 is the reserved-area hole;
+        //  entry 1 is the first dirty run until paging I/O removes it.
+        //
+
+        while (FatGetNextMcbEntry( Vcb, &Vcb->DirtyFatMcb, 1,
+                                   &Vbo, &Lbo, &ByteCount )) {
+
+            NT_ASSERT( Lbo == Vbo );
+            Offset.QuadPart = Vbo;
+            if (Offset.QuadPart < FlushedThrough || ByteCount == 0) {
+
+                // An overlapping write may still be in flight. Do not
+                // spin under the volume lock or declare the volume clean.
+                Status = STATUS_DEVICE_BUSY;
+                break;
+            }
+
+            FlushedThrough = Offset.QuadPart + ByteCount;
+            CcFlushCache( &Vcb->SectionObjectPointers, &Offset, ByteCount, &Iosb );
+            Status = Iosb.Status;
+            if (!NT_SUCCESS(Status)) {
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
     if (!NT_SUCCESS(Status)) {
 
         ReturnStatus = Status;
